  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nNo significant changes to the forecast were made in this cycle. \nDeterministic and ensemble guidance alike continue to be locked into \na persistent upper-level ridge through about midweek next week. As \nsuch, heat has remained the primary forecast concern for the time \nbeing. A trough over the Pacific Northwest will be one to watch as \nthe thermal ridge erodes and the trough slides to the east. \nThis may present a few opportunities for rain midweek next week \nbut more on that later...\n\n12Z upper-air observations clearly portray the ridge sprawled over \nthe southern and central CONUS with a largely sedentary low over the \nPacific Northwest and another in the vicinity of Newfoundland and \nLabrador. Our position relative to this Omega-block will allow \ntemperatures to once again soar into the mid/upper 90s areawide, \nwith high temperatures in our far south potentially reaching triple \ndigits and heat index values around 105. With little to no change in \nthe pattern on Saturday, expect similar conditions, just maybe a few \ndegrees warmer than today. Late Saturday night, a backdoor cold \nfront is expected to begin moving into the northeast portion of the \nCWA and continue sliding south throughout the day on Sunday. It is \npossible that some showers may be able to develop in the vicinity of \nthis boundary so have bumped PoPs into the 20-30% range across \nnorthern Missouri and into the 10-20% range south of US-36 Sunday \nafternoon and evening. Counties generally along and north of the \nGrand River will have the Extreme Heat Warning end Saturday night as \nslightly cooler conditions in the low/mid 90s are expected on Sunday \nwith the passage of the backdoor cold front and associated cloud \ncover/rain chances. South of this corridor, little reprieve is \nexpected on Sunday as the front is not anticipated to move through \nthe south and western portions of the CWA until after peak heating \nso the Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect along and south of the \nMissouri River until Tuesday evening. More marginal conditions are \nexpected on Monday behind the backdoor cold front, but several \nlocations are still expected to reach hazardous heat thresholds, \nespecially those around the KC Metro area and in east-central KS and \nwest-central MO. \n\nThe deamplifying ridge will be hurried west ahead of the then \nprogressive PNW low sometime midweek. Currently, forecast \ntemperatures and heat index values on Wednesday would qualify for an \nextension of the Extreme Heat Warning. However, there is substantial \nuncertainty regarding the timing of the attendant cold front that is \nset to sweep across the Plains. The most recent guidance suggests \nthat this could occur as early as Tuesday night or as late as \nWednesday night. Though an extension was considered during this \nforecast cycle, uncertainty was far too high for one to be issued. \nTrends will be monitored closely in the days ahead to make this \ndetermination. Rain opportunities may also arrive with this cold \nfront, but again, there is a lack of precision when it comes to \ntiming so have retained about 20-30% 6-hourly PoPs from \nWednesday morning into Friday morning to account for\nprecipitation potential from the cold front as well as any\nshortwaves that ripple through a comparatively more zonal\npattern.",
